e/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`\n37\t(owning team \"RHOAI Serving\"); Platform Operator and Documentation teams are\n38\tassumed from the component names (see Health Warnings).\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 KServe image remediation] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 Evidence package]\n45\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 Platform Operator image remediation] --> E003\n46\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 CI FIPS regression gate]\n47\t    E002 --> E004\n48\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 FIPS documentation]\n49\t    E002 --> E005\n50\t    E003 --> E005\n51\t    E004 --> E005\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t## DAG Justification\n55\t\n56\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n57\t|------|------|-----------|\n58\t| E001 → E003 | 13 (work-product dependency) | The evidence package's content (module inventory, build attestations, FIPS test results for the KServe images) is produced by the KServe remediation; the package cannot be assembled before the remediated images exist. |\n59\t| E002 → E003 | 13 (work-product dependency) | Same as above for the Platform Operator images in scope. |\n60\t| E001 → E004 | 13 (build-against artifact) | The CI gate's deny-list of non-validated crypto usage is derived from the module inventory established during KServe remediation. |\n61\t| E002 → E004 | 13 (build-against artifact) | Same as above for Platform Operator builds; the gate covers all serving-stack builds. |\n62\t| E001 → E005 | 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n63\t| E002 → E005 | 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n64\t| E003 → E005 | 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n65\t| E004 → E005 | 11 (docs last) | Same. Edges into the docs-authoring epic do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n66\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | 14 (different repos, parallel) | KServe and Platform Operator images live in different repos with no shared artifacts; the remediations run in parallel. |\n67\t| No onboarding chain | 20 (not applicable) | KServe is an in-platform component (architecture context exists, Konflux onboarded). Platform Operator is an existing canonical component in `.context/rhai-components.txt` whose images the strategy remediates — this is hardening of existing images, not provisioning of a new component (see Health Warnings). |\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n70\t\n71\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Priority-split analysis |\n72\t|-----|----------|---------|-------------------------|\n73\t| HLR-1 (serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001, RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Split by component/team boundary (Rule 1): KServe images and Platform Operator images live in different repos owned by different teams. |\n74\t| HLR-2 (compliance evidence package assembled and submitted) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Single cross-component work product; assigned to the serving-stack team with component KServe as the most-relevant canonical name (spans KServe and Platform Operator — see component mapping note below). |\n75\t| HLR-3 (CI enforces FIPS regressions) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Split from HLR-1 as a distinct, deferrable feature: it edits CI pipeline configuration rather than image content, has independent user value (regression protection), and shares no data structure or build surface with the image remediation. |\n76\t| HLR-4 (documentation states FIPS posture and prerequisites) | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | Substantive new documentation topics (not incidental touch-ups) → separate docs-authoring epic (Rule on work-product type boundaries). Its P0 priority comes from the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not from HLR-4's P2 label. |\n77\t\n78\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n79\t\n80\t## Component Mapping Notes\n81\t\n82\t- `RHAISTRAT-9013-E003` (evidence package) spans the KServe and Platform\n83\t  Operator remediations; assigned canonical component `KServe` as the single\n84\t  most-relevant name for the serving stack.\n85\t- `RHAISTRAT-9013-E004` (CI gate) covers serving-stack builds across both\n86\t  components; assigned canonical component `KServe` as the most-relevant name.\n87\t- `RHAISTRAT-9013-E005` uses canonical component `Documentation`.\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n92\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no per-component change/owner detail in\n93\t  Affected Components, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n94\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded with inferred team\n95\t  assignments and ACs derived from the HLRs.\n96\t- **External schedule gates**: the accredited-lab turnaround (historically six\n97\t  to nine months) and security review board cadence are externally controlled\n98\t  and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are recorded as\n99\t  Risks/Notes on the evidence-package epic — not as acceptance criteria — and\n100\t  they gate the certification outcome, not any epic's start.\n101\t- **Platform Operator lacks an architecture-context file**: it is present in\n102\t  the canonical component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) and the\n103\t  strategy remediates its existing images, so it is treated as an existing\n104\t  in-platform component rather than triggering the new-component onboarding\n105\t  chain (Rule 20). Consequently `architecture_claims` is scored 0 for its epic.\n106\t- **Docs epic priority**: HLR-4 is P2, but the docs-authoring epic carries P0\n107\t  per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0).\n108\t  This is rule-mandated, not a priority inversion.\n109\t- **Re-split trigger (evidence epic)**: if module-inventory and test-result\n110\t  assembly for the evidence package outgrows roughly one sprint of effort\n111\t  (e.g. the image count grows substantially), split the assembly work from the\n112\t  submission/review-board coordination work.\n113\t\n114\t## Tiered Delivery\n115\t\n116\tThe external certification process does not gate any engineering epic: every\n117\tHLR (FIPS-capable images, evidence package submitted, CI enforcement,\n118\tdocumentation) is deliverable without the lab's outcome, and the certification\n119\tresult itself is outside the strategy's requirements. Engineering value\n120\t(remediated images, regression protection, documentation) therefore ships as\n121\tTier 1 independent of the lab schedule; no Tier 2 dependency-gated epics are\n122\tneeded and no DAG split is required. The only externally-gated item — the\n123\tcertification decision — is a post-submission outcome tracked as a risk, not\n124\tan epic.\n125\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bdb3cb3103d03576","agent_id":"aeaef9707407d607e","session_id":"349a626c-8ce8-443f-b6d9-a34677df2f24","uuid":"956b60b5-22ef-486c-8c50-ae079df6de3e","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:44:37.756Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bbb889e0003bfcb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
